1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,004
Objects need boundaries. Access modifiers draw those lines.

2
00:00:04,004 --> 00:00:07,330
Who can see this field? Who can call this method?

3
00:00:07,330 --> 00:00:10,181
Visibility is ownership encoded in syntax.

4
00:00:10,181 --> 00:00:14,321
Today we map private, package-private, protected, and public.

5
00:00:14,321 --> 00:00:17,986
Visibility is a design decision — not an afterthought.

6
00:00:17,986 --> 00:00:19,193
Episode Eleven.

7
00:00:19,193 --> 00:00:24,260
Access Modifiers — private, public, protected, package-private.

8
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:26,324
Four levels. Narrow to wide.

9
00:00:26,324 --> 00:00:28,240
private — only this class.

10
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:31,926
No modifier — package-private — same package only.

11
00:00:31,926 --> 00:00:34,579
protected — package plus subclasses.

12
00:00:34,579 --> 00:00:37,896
public — anyone. A promise. Use it carefully.

13
00:00:37,896 --> 00:00:41,802
Default to the narrowest visibility that still works.

14
00:00:41,802 --> 00:00:45,414
Widen only when a real collaborator needs access.

15
00:00:45,414 --> 00:00:48,059
private is your first encapsulation tool.

16
00:00:48,059 --> 00:00:51,027
Fields start private unless you have a reason.

17
00:00:51,027 --> 00:00:54,511
Helpers that are not part of the API stay private too.

18
00:00:54,511 --> 00:00:58,254
If everything is public, you have no boundary — only hope.

19
00:00:58,254 --> 00:01:00,060
Hope is not an architecture.

20
00:01:00,060 --> 00:01:02,512
Start private. Widen only with intent.

21
00:01:02,512 --> 00:01:04,433
Package-private is underrated.

22
00:01:04,433 --> 00:01:07,827
Same package collaboration without publishing an API.

23
00:01:07,827 --> 00:01:10,965
Great for internal helpers shared by a few types.

24
00:01:10,965 --> 00:01:15,447
When a type must leave the package — promote visibility intentionally.

25
00:01:15,447 --> 00:01:18,328
Accidental public is how APIs grow barnacles.

26
00:01:18,328 --> 00:01:21,146
Package-private keeps the neighborhood tidy.

27
00:01:21,146 --> 00:01:24,234
protected supports inheritance-aware extension.

28
00:01:24,234 --> 00:01:27,519
Subclasses can reach it — so can the same package.

29
00:01:27,519 --> 00:01:32,512
public is the stable contract. Every public method is a promise to maintain.

30
00:01:32,512 --> 00:01:36,060
In libraries, public surface area is a long-term cost.

31
00:01:36,060 --> 00:01:39,608
Architect tip — treat public like a published product.

32
00:01:39,608 --> 00:01:42,564
Every public method is a maintenance promise.

33
00:01:42,564 --> 00:01:44,025
Three common mistakes.

34
00:01:44,025 --> 00:01:48,276
One — public fields for convenience. Encapsulation dies quietly.

35
00:01:48,276 --> 00:01:51,332
Two — making every helper public just in case.

36
00:01:51,332 --> 00:01:55,716
Three — protected used as a lazy public for subclasses everywhere.

37
00:01:55,716 --> 00:01:59,967
Also — widening visibility to fix a test instead of redesigning.

38
00:01:59,967 --> 00:02:03,022
Fix the design — do not dissolve the boundary.

39
00:02:03,022 --> 00:02:06,428
Interview question — default access versus private?

40
00:02:06,428 --> 00:02:09,700
Default — package-private — same package sees it.

41
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:12,037
private — only the declaring class.

42
00:02:12,037 --> 00:02:15,844
Then — prefer narrowest visibility. Public is a contract.

43
00:02:15,844 --> 00:02:18,048
That answer shows API discipline.

44
00:02:18,048 --> 00:02:21,320
Visibility choices are architecture in miniature.

45
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:24,608
Visibility needs a home. Next — packages.

46
00:02:24,608 --> 00:02:26,694
Episode Twelve — packages.

47
00:02:26,694 --> 00:02:30,383
Namespaces, boundaries, and ownership on disk.

48
00:02:30,383 --> 00:02:31,506
See you there.
